"National Institute for Basic Biology / News (https://www.nibb.ac.jp/en/pressroom/news/2020/10/06.html)\nciteturn16view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nibb.ac.jp/en/pressroom/news/2020/10/06.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 274\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow the Venus Flytrap Counts - (https://www.uni-wuerzburg.de/en/news-and-events/news/detail/news/how-the-venus-flytrap-counts-1/)\nciteturn16view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.uni-wuerzburg.de/en/news-and-events/news/detail/news/how-the-venus-flytrap-counts-1/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 217\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDemystifying the Venus flytrap action potential - Hedrich - 2023 - New Phytologist - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.19113)\nciteturn16view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.19113\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://nph.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/nph.19113; Total lines: 450\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn16view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0960982222019959\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA single touch can provide sufficient mechanical stimulation to trigger Venus flytrap closure | PLOS Biology (https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371%2Fjournal.pbio.3000740)\nciteturn16view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn15view0\",\"pattern\":\"single deflection\"}); Redirected to URL: https://journals.plos.org/plosbiology/article?id=10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740; Total lines: 671\nL240: Considering angular rather than linear deflection allowed us to correct for differences in the contact height of the sensor probe relative to the constriction site of the sensory hair as well as for different sensory-hair geometries (Figs cite87†1B , cite88†2C and 2D ). Therefore, a single deflection can be approximated by a discrete increase in the angular displacement, and the triggering of an AP mostly depends on the magnitude of the angular displacement.\nL241: We defined a single deflection as the combination of a back-and-forth angular displacement, similar to what happens when an animal touches the hair. Each measurement consisted of two subsequent deflections with a 1-s gap between them, up to a predefined angular displacement θ. If the trap did not close, we waited for 2 min to make sure that the RP was completely reset.\nL242: Since RP measurements are destructive and not compatible with our noninvasive in vivo experiments, we relied on data from the literature [cite84†10 , cite85†19 ]. The duration of this waiting period was chosen because a series of earlier experiments had demonstrated that, at temperatures below 30°C, two deflections within 30–40 s were necessary to rapidly and completely close the trap.\nL253: Based on these findings, we developed a simple model to explore the limits of angular displacement and velocity within which the traps would react. In our ECB model, a mechanical deflection leads to a charge buildup of the RP as a function of the angular velocity ω and the displacement θ, while the charges continuously dissipate. If the accumulated charges surpass a certain threshold value cite103†Image , an AP is elicited.\nL254: Additionally, we implemented a refractory period t_{RP}, representing the time interval required before another AP can be induced [cite104†27 , cite105†28 ]. In the Venus flytrap, subsequent APs separated as close as 0.75–1 s were reported [cite81†16 , cite102†26 ].\nL255: The model reproduced the experimentally found bounds for double deflections and predicted that if the deflections are too fast and/or too small, more than two deflections might be required to trigger trap closure (red area in cite106†Fig 3A ) because a single deflection is not sufficient to elicit an AP (cite106†Fig 3B ), something we also observed in our experiments (cite87†Fig 1C , middle). Similarly, the model showed that very low angular velocities (ω< 0.04 rad s^{−1}) cannot fill up the RP.\nL256: Unexpectedly, the model predicted a range of intermediate angular velocities (0.04 rad s^{−1} <ω< 10 rad s^{−1}), at which a single deflection is sufficient to elicit the two or more APs that are necessary to initiate trap closure (cite106†Fig 3B ).\nL257: Download:\nL258: \nL259:   * cite107†PNG larger image L260:   * cite108†TIFF original image L261: \nL262: Fig 3. ECB model.\nL263: (A) Prediction of the number of action potentials triggered for a single deflection with consecutive waiting time as function of angular displacement (“disp.”) θ and velocity ω, confronted with experimental observations for cases in which trap closure was triggered by single (blue circles) and double deflections (green circles).\nL264: (B) Number of deflections necessary to trigger trap closure with phases of one, two, or three deflections compared to the findings for single- (blue circles) and double-deflection (green circles) experiments. For the underlying data, see sheet Figs 3A and B in cite66†S1 Data . ECB, electromechanical charge buildup.\nL265: cite109†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.g003†doi.org L266: ### A single deflection at intermediate angular velocity triggers trap closure\nL267: As single-deflection trap triggering is contrary to the accepted view, we wanted to experimentally test this prediction of the model. Indeed, we observed trap closure induced by single deflections at lower angular velocities (cite110†S2 Video ). To narrow down the range in which this occurs, we repeatedly deflected the same sensory hair with varying angular velocities until the trap closed. Between two consecutive deflections, we waited 2 min for the trap to recover and any RP to dissipate.\nL268: The lower boundary of the angular velocity ω required for single-deflection closure was determined by incrementally increasing it after every deflection (n = 17). An initial velocity below 0.009 rad s^{−1} was chosen, which never resulted in trap closure. Subsequent single deflections were performed at increasing velocities until the trap shut (cite111†Fig 4A ).\nL269: The higher boundary was similarly determined by starting with a velocity ω > 3 rad s^{−1}, followed by a stepwise decrease until the trap shut (n = 9).\nL270: Additionally, we performed another set of single-deflection experiments (n = 5), in which the speed of the force probe was kept constant, leading to an intermediate angular velocity between 0.2 and 0.4 rad s^{−1}, while the angular displacement θ was gradually increased during subsequent deflections in order to get the lower boundary of θ required to trigger trap closure by a single deflection.\nL271: Download:\nL272: \nL273:   * cite112†PNG larger image L274:   * cite113†TIFF original image L275: \nL276: Fig 4. Slow single deflections lead to trap closure.\nL277: (A) Successive single displacements (“disp.”) of the sensory hair with increasing angular velocity while the torque is recorded. (B) Individual single-deflection experiments showing the stimulus that initiated trap closure (green circles) and the preceding, nontriggering deflection (red triangles). The specific experiments shown in (A), (C), and (D) are indicated.\nL278: (C and D) Single deflections at an intermediate velocity that led to trap triggering where either (C) two APs were generated during bending (on the way in), or (D) one AP is fired at the beginning of bending (on the way in) and a second at the end of the return (on the way out). Only the data up to the start of the trap closure were used. The data affected by motion artifacts are indicated by dotted lines. For the underlying data, see sheets Fig 4A to Fig 4D in cite66†S1 Data . AP, action potential.\nL279: cite114†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.g004†doi.org L280: All the single deflections resulting in trap closure, together with the preceding stimuli for which no trap closure was observed, define the region where a single deflection triggers closure (cite111†Fig 4B and cite100†S1C Fig ). The model output for single-deflection triggers covers a similar parameter space as experimentally obtained for angular deflection θ versus angular velocity ω.\nL281: We observed that a single deflection can trigger trap closure at intermediate angular velocities of the deflection (0.03 rad s^{−1} ≤ ω ≤ 4 rad s^{−1}) but is not sufficient at slower or faster angular velocities (see cite115†S2 Fig for experimental data on single and double deflections). Incorporating the single-deflection experiments into the model provides a better prediction of the area in which a single touch can lead to two APs and thus trap closure (cite106†Fig 3A ).\nL282: In the slow-velocity deflection experiments, we were able to determine whether trap triggering happened while advancing (n = 6) or retracting (n = 11) the force probe. Our results indicate that both back and forward displacements contribute to the overall RP level. AP measurements disclosed how these single deflections lead to trap closure.\nL283: When the trap closed during the advancement of the force probe, two APs, one shortly after the other, were observed during the bending of the hair (advancing the probe) (cite111†Fig 4C and cite116†S3 Video ). In the second case (triggering during retraction), one AP was fired during initial bending and a second while retracting the sensor probe as the hair returned to its original position (cite111†Fig 4D ). In both cases, the second AP led to immediate trap closure.\nL284: ### Quantification of the trap snap forces and torques\nL285: In addition to the deflection parameters, we also measured the snap force developed by the triggered trap (cite117†Fig 5A ). A median snap force F_{close} of 73 mN was determined from 48 different traps (cite117†Fig 5B ), ranging from 18 to 174 mN in extreme cases.\nL286: These values are slightly lower than the previously published 140–150 mN measured with a piezoelectric sensor film [cite118†29 , cite119†30 ]; however, we measured the force at the beginning of snapping while Volkov and colleagues measured the impact of the lobe rims.\nL287: Because the measured force strongly depends on the force sensor's position, as well as on the orientation and size of the leaf, the closing torque around the midrib τ_{close} with a median of 0.65 mN m is a better quantity to characterize the trap's closing force (cite117†Fig 5C ).\nL288: The delay time, i.e., the time between the mechanical stimulus and the start of trap closure, was 0.6 ± 0.3 s (mean ± standard deviation, n = 18) based on video analysis, which is in the same range as the 0.4 s previously reported [cite120†31 ].\nL335: We defined a single deflection as a combination of back-and-forth angular displacement of the sensory hair caused by the advance and return of the force probe in x direction (i.e., parallel to the midrib). This movement is parallel to the force-sensitive direction of the force probe and also ensures a perpendicular contact between force probe and sensory hair.\nL336: The velocity of the sensor probe v during deflection can be defined, and either a maximum linear displacement d or a maximum force F is set to define the maximum deflection (cite88†Fig 2D ). The position control was used for the double deflections, where the angular displacement had to be increased incrementally between subsequent experiments. The force feedback mode was used in the single-deflection experiments to ensure maximum sensory-hair displacement without damaging the deflection force sensor.\nL392: As a matter of fact, even the strongest possible desensitization that only depends on the charge of the RP cannot avoid multiple firing. Several procedures for additional desensitization in form of Q_{STM} feedback or as function of n_{AP} were implemented but, because of a lack of experimental insight, not further explored. Note that the single-deflection zone must be bounded by the multiple-deflection zones.\nL401: ### cite169†S1 Fig. Double-deflection experiments.\nL402: \nL403: (A) Torque versus angular velocity plot for the double-deflection experiments shown in cite87†Fig 1B . (B) Visualization of the force needed to sufficiently deflect a hair for double-deflection triggering. (C) Torque versus angular velocity plot for the single-deflection experiments shown in cite111†Fig 4B . For the underlying data, see sheets S1A Fig and S1C Fig in cite66†S1 Data .\nL404: \nL405: cite170†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s001†doi.org L406: \nL407: (EPS)\nL408: ### cite171†S2 Fig. Summary of all the mechanical stimuli that resulted in trap closure.\nL409: \nL410: For the underlying data, see sheet S2 Fig in cite66†S1 Data .\nL411: \nL412: cite172†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s002†doi.org L413: \nL414: (EPS)\nL415: ### cite173†S3 Fig. Single deflections without any prestimulation of the sensory hairs can lead to trap closure.\nL416: \nL417: (A-B) The traps triggered during bending (on the way in) and (C-D) triggered at the end of the return (on the way out). These experiments did not have preceding, nontriggering deflections and were therefore not included in cite111†Fig 4B . For the underlying data, see sheets S3A Fig to S3D Fig in cite66†S1 Data .\nL418: \nL419: cite174†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s003†doi.org L420: \nL421: (EPS)\nL422: ### cite175†S1 Video. Double deflection of a sensory hair leading to two APs and trap closure.\nL423: \nL424: The sensor-probe position (motion in x direction), the force at the deflection force sensor, and the measured APs are displayed next to the movie (cite87†Fig 1C is from this movie). AP, action potential.\nL425: \nL426: cite176†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s004†doi.org L427: \nL428: (AVI)\nL429: ### cite177†S2 Video. Single deflection of a sensory hair leading to trap closure.\nL430: At slow angular velocities, a single deflection is sufficient to trigger trap closure. The trap shown was not prestimulated, demonstrating that single-deflection closure is solely depending on the angular velocity at a sufficiently large deflection and is not the result of a summation effect due to previous subthreshold stimuli. This video also demonstrates that the sensory hair bends mainly at the constriction whereas the lever is hardly deformed.\nL431: The trap does not move when the sensory hair is deflected.\nL432: cite178†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s005†doi.org L433: \nL434: (AVI)\nL435: ### cite179†S3 Video. Single deflection of a sensory hair leading to two APs shortly after one another during initial bending, resulting in trap closure.\nL436: \nL437: The sensor-probe position (motion in x direction), the force at the deflection force sensor, and the measured APs are displayed next to the movie (cite111†Fig 4C is from this movie). AP, action potential.\nL438: \nL439: cite180†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s006†doi.org L440: \nL441: (AVI)\nL442: ### cite181†S1 Data. Raw data.\nL443: \nL444: This file contains all the raw data underlying the plots presented in Figs cite87†1 , cite106†3 –cite117†5 , and cite100†S1 –cite128†S3 Figs.\nL445: \nL446: cite182†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pbio.3000740.s007†doi.org L447: \nL448: (XLSX)\nL449: \nL450: ## Acknowledgments\nL451: \nL452: We would like to thank Karl Huwiler and Christian Frey for taking care of the Venus flytraps and providing us with healthy plants.\nL453: ## References\nL454:   1. 1. Letter no. 8773 [Internet]. Darwin Correspondence Project. 1873 [cited 2019 Jul 4]. Available from: cite183†http://www.darwinproject.ac.uk/DCP-LETT-8773†www.darwinproject.ac.uk L455:   2. 2. Edwards ST. Dionaea muscipula. Venus’s fly-trap. Curtis’s Bot Mag. 1804;20(3):254–255.\nL456:      * cite56†View Article L457:      * cite184†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L458:   3. 3. Nuttall T. The genera of North American plants: and a catalogue of the species, to the year 1817. Philadelphia: Printed for the Author by D. Heartt; 1818.\nL459:   4. 4. Curtis MA. Enumeration of plants growing spontaneously around Wilmington, North Carolina, with remarks on some new and obscure species. Boston J Nat Hist. 1834;1:82–141.\nL460:      * cite56†View Article L461:      * cite185†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDemystifying the Venus flytrap action potential - Hedrich - 2023 - New Phytologist - Wiley Online Library (https://doi.org/10.1111/nph.19113)\nciteturn16view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn15view2\",\"pattern\":\"calcium\"}); Redirected to URL: https://nph.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/nph.19113; Total lines: 450\nL125: The flytrap recognizes collisions with living prey or touch by experimenters with the help of trigger hairs emerging from the inner trap surface. These sensory organs are equally spaced, three on each trap lobe (Fig. cite30†1a ). A 3° displacement of a trigger hair by a force of 30 μN – equivalent to the power of a mosquito – is sufficient to provoke an AP (Scherzer et al., cite31†2019 ). The first AP, elicited by the initial touch deflection of a single trigger hair, is memorized for c. 20 s.\nL126: If the same or another trigger hair is stimulated within these 20 s to bring a second AP on its way, the trap closes within 0.1 s. Remarkably, a calcium wave travels together and with the same fast speed as the AP (Fig. cite30†1b ). Both signals propagate with up to 10 cm s^{−1} along both trap lobes, a velocity unrivalled by other plant systems.\nL127: The cytosolic Ca^{2+} transients of the first and second wave sum up, and upon reaching a critical threshold, they induce the release of viscoelastic energy stored in the open trap, making the trap snap shut (Suda et al., cite32†2020 ). While the entrapped prey tries to escape, it will keep colliding with the trigger hairs and thus sustains AP firing.\nL128: After having counted and integrated ‘2’ in the process of AP-dependent trap snapping, the following three to five or more APs are translated into jasmonate (JA) biosynthesis and signalling (Böhm et al., cite33†2016 ).\nL129: While the fast trap closure does not require JA, the touch hormone stimulates slow hermetical sealing of the capture organ and the activation of glands that cover the inner surface, which leads to the secretion of digestive enzymes into the newly formed green stomach (Scherzer et al., cite34†2017 ).\nL130: Fig. 1\nL131: \nL132: cite16†Open in figure viewer cite35†PowerPoint L133: (a) Open Dionaea muscipula trap. Trigger hairs located at the inner trap surface serve as mechanosensors: their displacement provokes the action potential. (b) Upon touch perception, calcium waves and action potentials (APs) are released and together propagate along both trap lobes.\nL134: (c) Dionaea's AP consists of five distinct phases: after an initial Ca^{2+} influx via DmGLR3.6, the Ca^{2+} signal is amplified by a Ca^{2+}-induced Ca^{2+} release (CICR) from the endoplasmic reticulum to an all-or-nothing cytosolic Ca^{2+} transient (phase 1). If the Ca^{2+} level passes the threshold, Cl^{−} release via Ca^{2+}-dependent ALMT anion channels triggers an initial, rapid depolarization (phase 2).\nL135: Positive of −60 mV, depolarization-dependent DmSKOR K^{+} channels and DmAHA4 H^{+} pumps are activated and slow the depolarization kinetics (1^{st} shoulder). At c. −20 mV, when anion efflux and K^{+} efflux compensate each other, the depolarization maximum is reached. The repolarization (phase 3) sets in when [Ca^{2+}]_{cytosolic} drops, the anion channel inactivates, and DmSKOR and DmAHA4 allow K^{+} and H^{+} release from the cell.\nL136: Negative of −60 mV DmSKOR inactivates, slowing the fast, initial repolarization (2^{nd} shoulder). The delayed deactivation of the depolarization-activated H^{+} pump results in sustained H^{+} export, thus provoking a transient overshoot hyperpolarization (phase 4). During membrane hyperpolarization, DmAHA4 voltage inactivates and H^{+}-driven DmHAK5 and NRT-type anion transporters reset the pre-AP resting potential by recharging the K^{+} and anion battery (phase 5).\nL153: During repolarization, negative from −60 mV DmSKOR inactivates, the depolarization-activated H^{+} pump takes over and causes a transient overshoot hyperpolarization (phase 4). During membrane hyperpolarization, DmAHA4 voltage inactivates and the H^{+}-driven DmHAK5 as well as NRT-type anion transporters refill the K^{+} and A^{−} battery and reset the pre-AP resting potential (phase 5; Iosip et al., cite45†2020 ; Scherzer et al., cite38†2022a ).\nL154: Compared with the classical nerve and muscle AP that is based on a voltage-dependent Na^{+} channel for depolarization and a K^{+} channel for repolarization, the Dionaea AP is much more complex (please see also cite46†https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5Ri4VNX56CA†www.youtube.com ). Most importantly, the flytrap AP and that of Mimosa (see below) strictly depend on calcium.\nL155: Rather than being governed by a receptor potential threshold, like in the nerve/muscle AP, the initiation of a propagating electrical signals in plants is triggered when a critical cytoplasmic Ca^{2+} threshold is passed.\nL156: ## III. AP is a feature of all fast-moving plants\nL157: Like the Venus flytrap, touch-sensitive Mimosa pudica plants can fire an all-or-nothing AP and can move their leaves reasonably fast with 1.5 cm s^{−1} (Volkov et al., cite47†2010 ). In this system, a Ca^{2+} wave propagates along the same route and at the same speed as the AP, too (Hagihara et al., cite48†2022 ). This documents that the calcium and the electrical signal are two sides of the same coin.\nL158: The Fabacea Mimosa pudica grows complex leaves with the petioles containing small pinnules, arranged on the rachis or midrib of the pinna. A pulvinus at the base of a plant leaf or leaflet facilitates thigmonastic movements. Upon touch or wounding such as flame stimulation of a leaflet, the Ca^{2+}-electrical signals propagate the pinnules from the top of the rachis to the petiole, stem and along the stem.\nL159: Thus, in contrast to the situation in Dionaea, where the AP is exclusively a trap feature, the electrical signal in Mimosa is spreading over the entire shoot. How can an electrical signal in Dionaea traps and the Mimosa shoot move so fast? Long-distance, high-speed communication in plants takes advantage of the vascular networks.\nL179: What do we know about the receptor Ca^{2+} channels involved? In Arabidopsis, OSCAs have been identified as osmo/mechano-sensitive Ca^{2+} channels (Yuan et al., cite68†2014 ; Murthy et al., cite69†2018 ), and in the trigger hair of the Venus flytrap, an OSCA1.7 is very likely involved in hapto-calcium-electrical signal transduction (Procko et al., cite70†2021 ; Scherzer et al., cite40†2022b ). The nature of the cold and the heat receptor channel is still unknown, but they very likely represent independent entities.\nL229:   * Gao YQ, Jimenez-Sandoval P, Tiwari S, Stolz S, Wang J, Glauser G, Santiago J, Farmer EE. 2023. Ricca's factors as mobile proteinaceous effectors of electrical signaling. Cell 186: 1337–1351.\nL